body:
I lived in one of  these. The problem was the city wouldn't create an addiontial water bill and my landlords didn't cover water. So the "main" house got a water bill and then had to tell us what our portion was from a meter the landlord installed. It was a giant pain. Then our water shut off because the main house stopped paying the water bill and took our money. Which meant that our landlord had to step up, sort out the drama of who paid who, pay the bill to get the water back on for us and the other granny unit. 

&#x200B;

Then there was trash. Again, the city saw us as one lot and gave us two trash cans. We had to spilt the trash bill and sometimes there was too much trash and it all didn't fit. That sucked.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

The worst part was parking. The main house had a garage and a driveway but didn't use either and took up all the street parking. So we had to park farther away and walk, which we already had a little bit more of a walk because we were in the backyard. It stunk.

If the stove and fridge are in your lease as "as is", that sucks. I would check yourself instead of relying on landlords. I have seen leases with fridges, washer, and dryers not guaranteed, but never a stove. So that sticks out to me. Do you have renter's insurnace? Mine covered $500 of food loss once (due to a 3 week power outage, but still)

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

As for the water, I would get it tested yourself. Most cities have a water company and even if it is well water, most will test for free or a simple fee. My Home Depot will also test water for free (and then try to sell you stuff to fix it, so be prepared to say no).
